## The segment {#sec-segment}

In chapter II of his prize essay on the tides [@eulerE57] Euler asks by what force the sun or the moon tends to
move a particle of the earth relative to the earth's centre. He answers on seven pages, 33 to 39 of the volume
of the Opera omnia: a principle at the end of §22, a figure in §24, the force resolved exactly in §§25 and 26,
expanded in a small ratio in §27, and read for its consequences in §§28 and 29. An earlier paper treated this
argument briefly as one of two cases [@grasshoff2026twocases]. Here it is the only subject, and the aim is
different: to set Euler's own words beside the reconstruction, passage by passage, so that a reader can see
for every sentence what has been made of it.

The method is that of the series [@grasshoff2026fundamentals; @grasshoff2026astra]. The *model* is written
out: the points of Euler's figure with their constructions, the lengths as symbols, the forces as quantities
defined on them. The *argument* is written as steps, one proposition each, with the premises it is drawn from
and the rule that licenses it. A program *checks* whether every premise is there, whether every proposition
holds in the model for every admissible value and position, whether every step is an instance of its rule,
and whether the conclusion rests only on what is cited.

The figure is drawn here from the model, after Euler's Fig. 3: the earth as a circle about C, the luminary S, the
particle M with its perpendicular MP on CS. It moves: the angle φ at C, which fixes the place of M, runs through a
full turn. The distance of S is a drawing value, as it is in the printed figure.

{{< famulus fr:astra/euler-fig-3 animation >}}

## How to read the passages {#sec-reading}

The segment is divided into twelve passages. A passage is a stretch of Euler's text that does one thing: states
the principle, sets up the figure, gives a formula, draws a consequence. Each passage has three parts.

*The source.* Euler's text, in Latin as the edition transcribes it, or in English. A mark such as |35| shows
where a page of the Opera omnia begins. The English is the edition's stored page translation, a machine
translation; it was compared with the Latin for this paper and is a working translation, not a published one.
Three wordings were corrected, all three at a page break, where a translation made page by page had broken a
sentence in two.

*The reconstruction.* The step or steps of the argument that say what the passage says. A step has a number, a
claim in words, the proposition in the notation of the figure, the steps or constructions it is drawn from,
the rule, and a mark: ✓ an instance of the rule named, ◦ read off the construction, ≈ by calculation or by
expansion. Where a passage is not a step, the reconstruction says what it is instead: a definition in the
model, or a part of the text that is not reconstructed.

*The notes.* What the reconstruction supplies that Euler leaves unsaid, where it departs from his route, and
what the check found.

::: {.content-visible when-format="html"}
In this version the passages are interactive. *Latin · English* switches the source. *Overview · Steps · Full*
sets how much of the reconstruction is shown; every step also opens by itself. *Notes* adds the third column.
Selecting a step shows in the figure what it speaks of. *Open in the edition* opens the page of the Opera omnia
in the edition's viewer, in a separate window.
:::

::: {.content-visible unless-format="html"}
In print the source stands in two columns, Latin and English, with the reconstruction and the notes below. The
page version of this issue shows the same passages as an interactive page: the source beside the
reconstruction, the notes as a third column that can be switched on, a figure that shows what a selected step
speaks of, and for every passage a link that opens the page of the Opera omnia in the edition's viewer.
:::

## The passages {#sec-passages}

{{< famulus fr:astra/euler-e57-tide exposition >}}

## What the reconstruction shows {#sec-results}

*The principle is a definition.* The sentence at the end of §22 is not a premise among others. It fixes what
the argument is about: the force on the particle less the force on the centre. In the model it is the
definition of the two quantities observed, along MC and along MR, and every later step is a statement about
them.

*The exact components are confirmed, by another route than Euler's.* §26 reaches the two components through a
perpendicular QV and the similar triangles QVM and MPC. The reconstruction does not rebuild this. It states
that Euler's closed expressions equal the quantities defined, and the check confirms it in every sampled
configuration. This is the widest gap between the source and the reconstruction, and the notes say so at the
passage.

*The expansion has an order, and the order can be tested.* §27 keeps first two terms and then one. A step of
this kind is not an equality. It is checked by making the ratio of the particle's distance to the luminary's
distance small, a hundredth, a thousandth, a ten-thousandth, and requiring the relative difference to fall
like the stated power of the ratio.

*An emendation of the editor is wrong.* For the component along MR the Opera omnia print 4xx + yy in the term
of second order, with the note "Editio princeps et Manuscriptus: (4xx − yy)/2a loco (4xx + yy)/2a. Correxit
EJA." (@fig-note). The reconstruction follows the first edition and the manuscript. Their reading follows from
Euler's own substitution three lines above, and it is of second order: its relative difference from the exact
component is 1.8 · 10⁻⁴, 1.8 · 10⁻⁶ and 1.8 · 10⁻⁸ at the three ratios. The emended reading is of first order
only: 2.0 · 10⁻², 2.0 · 10⁻³ and 2.0 · 10⁻⁴. The emendation does not touch Euler's results, which use the first
term. We have not seen the first edition or the manuscript; their reading is known to us from the editor's
note.

![Opera omnia II 31, p. 37 (`fr:enestroem/E57/0018`): the component along MR with the emended sign, the
first-order formulae, and note 5.](figures/euler-p37-formula-note.png){#fig-note width=92%}

*A rounded angle.* §28 gives the angle whose tangent is √2 as 54°45′ "circiter". To the nearest minute it is
54°44′.

*What is not reconstructed.* The first resolution of §25, along CS and along MP with the diagonal MO, is left
out: §26 replaces it, and no formula of §§26 to 29 cites it. Euler's Fig. 4 is not drawn. The checks report the
positions of the particle on the line CS as not covered by the construction of the perpendicular MP.

## The exposition as a format {#sec-format}

The page is built from one file that names, for every passage, where it begins and where it ends on a page of
the edition, which steps reconstruct it, and the notes. Nothing of the source is typed into that file. The
builder takes the Latin from the page of the edition and the English from the stored translation, and it
refuses to build when a passage is not found exactly once, when a step of the argument stands beside no
passage, or when a passage lies in no paragraph of the reference register. A correction of the translation is
listed with its reason and shown as a correction. The reference of the source comes from a checked record.

The same file gives the interactive page and the print, so the two cannot drift apart. The procedure for
another source is the same: choose the segment, write the model, write the argument, divide the text into
passages, attach the steps, and annotate what remains.

## Limits {#sec-limits}

The Latin is the edition's transcription, not a new collation; one spelling of the transcription (*Praetera*)
is kept as it stands. The English is a machine translation compared with the Latin by the author, not a
scholarly translation. The reconstruction covers what Euler argues in these sections, not what he presupposes
from the sections before: that the earth may be treated as if collected in its centre (the paragraph before §22), and the form of
the force. The emendation is judged on the argument alone, without the first edition or the manuscript.

## Availability {.unnumbered}

The exposition is `expositions/euler-e57-tide.yaml` in the Famulus workspace `famulus-astronomica`; the model,
the derivation, the tested emendation and the figure are in `examples/euler/`. The page element is
`<astra-exposition>`. Euler is cited by the pages of the Famulus collection `enestroem`, document `E57`; page n
of the collection is page n + 19 of Opera omnia II 31.
